{"qid":"Q1337854","names":["Emirate of Córdoba"],"title":"Emirate of Córdoba","endonym":{"native":"Emirato de Córdoba","lang":"es"},"capsule":"The Emirate of Córdoba was an independent Muslim state on the Iberian Peninsula, established in 756 when the Umayyad prince Abd al-Rahman I escaped the Abbasid massacre of his dynasty and seized al-Andalus, refusing to recognize Abbasid suzerainty. Under Abd al-Rahman II (r. 822–852) the emirate reached administrative maturity, with Córdoba developing into the largest city in western Europe, famed for its libraries, mosques, and court culture. The emirate's formal title was transformed in 929 when Abd al-Rahman III proclaimed himself caliph, upgrading the state to the Caliphate of Córdoba; the dataset's attestation to 935 reflects this transitional period.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The emirate's administrative boundaries in al-Andalus are documented through Arab geographical texts (Ibn Hawqal, al-Idrisi's predecessors) and Christian chronicles; the northern frontier with the Christian kingdoms fluctuated and is approximated from military campaign records.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Emirate of Córdoba","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_C%C3%B3rdoba"},{"label":"Britannica: Córdoba (Spain)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Cordoba-Spain"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Emirate of Córdoba","rulers":[{"qid":"Q29000","name":"Abd ar-Rahman I","start":756,"end":788,"image":"Abd ar-Rahman I.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Abd%20ar-Rahman%20I.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{